[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Synchronization Primitives: Mutexes, Semaphores, and Condition Variables"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In concurrent programming, multiple threads or processes often need to access shared resources. Without proper synchronization, this can lead to race conditions, data corruption, and other unpredictable behavior. Synchronization primitives are tools that help coordinate access to shared resources, ensuring data consistency and program correctness. This tutorial explores three fundamental synchronization primitives: mutexes, semaphores, and condition variables."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Mutexes (Mutual Exclusion Locks)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A mutex is a locking mechanism that ensures only one thread can access a critical section of code at a time. It provides exclusive access to a shared resource. Think of it as a key to a room; only the thread holding the key (the mutex) can enter the room (the critical section)."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The two primary operations on a mutex are:"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lock (Acquire): A thread attempts to acquire the mutex. If the mutex is currently unlocked, the thread acquires it and proceeds. If the mutex is locked, the thread blocks (waits) until the mutex becomes available."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unlock (Release): A thread releases the mutex, allowing another waiting thread to acquire it."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// Example using pthreads (POSIX Threads) in C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

pthread_mutex_t mutex;
int shared_data = 0;

void* thread_function(void* arg) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); // Acquire the mutex
  shared_data++;
  printf("Thread ID: %ld, Shared Data: %d\n", (long)pthread_self(), shared_data);
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Release the mutex
  return NULL;
}

int main() {
  pthread_t thread1, thread2;
  pthread_mutex_init(&mutex, NULL);

  pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, thread_function, NULL);
  pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, thread_function, NULL);

  pthread_join(thread1, NULL);
  pthread_join(thread2, NULL);

  pthread_mutex_destroy(&mutex);
  return 0;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Semaphores"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A semaphore is a more general synchronization primitive than a mutex. It maintains a counter that represents the number of available resources. Semaphores can be used to control access to a limited number of resources or to signal events between threads."},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The two primary operations on a semaphore are:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Wait (Decrement/P): Decrements the semaphore's counter. If the counter is positive, the thread proceeds. If the counter is zero, the thread blocks until the counter becomes positive."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Signal (Increment/V): Increments the semaphore's counter, potentially waking up a waiting thread."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"A binary semaphore is a semaphore with a counter that can only be 0 or 1. It effectively behaves like a mutex."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Example using semaphores in C (POSIX)
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

sem_t semaphore;
int shared_resource = 0;

void* thread_function(void* arg) {
    sem_wait(&semaphore); // Wait (decrement)
    printf("Thread %ld accessing shared resource\n", (long)pthread_self());
    shared_resource++; // Access shared resource
    printf("Thread %ld updated shared resource to %d\n", (long)pthread_self(), shared_resource);
    sem_post(&semaphore); // Signal (increment)
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t thread1, thread2;
    sem_init(&semaphore, 0, 1); // Initialize semaphore with initial value 1 (binary semaphore)

    pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, thread_function, NULL);
    pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, thread_function, NULL);

    pthread_join(thread1, NULL);
    pthread_join(thread2, NULL);

    sem_destroy(&semaphore);
    return 0;
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Condition Variables"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Condition variables are used to signal threads about changes in a shared state. They are always used in conjunction with a mutex. Condition variables allow threads to wait until a specific condition becomes true."},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary operations on a condition variable are:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Wait: Atomically releases the associated mutex and puts the thread to sleep. The thread is awakened when another thread signals the condition variable. Upon being awakened, the thread re-acquires the mutex."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Signal: Wakes up one thread that is waiting on the condition variable."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Broadcast: Wakes up all threads that are waiting on the condition variable."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"// Example using condition variables in C (POSIX)
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

pthread_mutex_t mutex;
pthread_cond_t condition;
int shared_data = 0;
bool data_ready = false;

void* producer_function(void* arg) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    shared_data = 42;
    data_ready = true;
    pthread_cond_signal(&condition); // Signal the consumer
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return NULL;
}

void* consumer_function(void* arg) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    while (!data_ready) {
        pthread_cond_wait(&condition, &mutex); // Wait for the signal
    }
    printf("Consumer: Received data: %d\n", shared_data);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t producer, consumer;
    pthread_mutex_init(&mutex, NULL);
    pthread_cond_init(&condition, NULL);

    pthread_create(&producer, NULL, producer_function, NULL);
    pthread_create(&consumer, NULL, consumer_function, NULL);

    pthread_join(producer, NULL);
    pthread_join(consumer, NULL);

    pthread_mutex_destroy(&mutex);
    pthread_cond_destroy(&condition);
    return 0;
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, mutexes provide exclusive access to shared resources, semaphores manage access to a limited number of resources or signal events, and condition variables allow threads to wait for specific conditions to become true. Choosing the right synchronization primitive depends on the specific requirements of your concurrent program."}]
